// Legal norms: ODRL 2.2, LegalRuleML 1.0 and LKIF Core 1.1: the standards' constraints, their sample data and the links between them
//
// Generated by ../../crosswalk.py from crosswalk.json, sample.json and the model.json and sample.json of each
// standard. Edit those and regenerate, rather than this file.
//
// Each standard's labels and relationship types are prefixed: ODRL 2.2 as odrl__, LegalRuleML 1.0 as lrml__, LKIF
// Core 1.1 as lkif__. The links between them are not.
//
// The residential tenancy of the LKIF Core sample, written in all three standards. The statute's obligation on
// landlords to keep the dwelling in repair, the tenant's permission to withhold rent once they have given notice of
// disrepair, and the prohibition on evicting without a court order are added as LegalRuleML deontic specifications,
// borne by the landlord and tenant and associated with the statute as their legal source and the legislature as their
// authority, and as an ODRL Set policy over the dwelling, where the notice becomes a duty to inform the landlord that
// the permission carries. The lease becomes an ODRL Agreement granting the tenant the use of the dwelling and the
// duty to pay rent. Sections 30 and 95 of the LegalRuleML sample's Residential Tenancies (Example) Act 2024 are added
// to ODRL in turn: the tenant's obligation to pay rent, with the late fee as its consequence, and the landlord's
// prohibition on entering the premises. Nodes that are the same individual carry the same IRI in every standard and
// are linked by SAME_AS; the late fee duty and the lease agreement are only RELATED_TO the LegalRuleML penalty
// statement and the LKIF contract. The ODRL sample's music licence describes nothing else here, so it is not linked.
// ODRL 2.2: A record label, Northwind Records, licenses an album and its four tracks. Its catalogue-wide Set policy
// prohibits modifying the recordings, with deleting modified copies as the remedy; an Offer inherits from it and lets
// anyone stream the album for a per-stream fee; and an Agreement with the streaming service Tunely inherits from the
// offer, permitting it to stream and play the album in the EU or the UK until the end of 2027, with duties to pay, to
// attribute the artist and to report usage monthly, and a prohibition on commercialising it. ODRL's actions, left
// operands and operators are nodes keyed by their ODRL IRIs; the companies and their IRIs are fictional. LegalRuleML
// 1.0: A LegalRuleML document modelling four sections of a fictional Residential Tenancies (Example) Act 2024 of
// Victoria. A strict rule obliges the tenant to pay rent on time, repaired by a late fee penalty; a defeasible rule
// obliges the landlord to give 28 days' notice to vacate, with two alternative readings, and is overridden by a
// defeasible rule permitting 14 days' notice where the premises are unfit; a further rule prohibits the landlord from
// entering without notice. Associations tie each statement to its section, the enacting authority, the jurisdiction
// and its in-force date, and the agents, figures and roles of the tenancy and of the drafting are recorded beside
// them. LKIF Core 1.1: A fictional residential tenancy, read through LKIF Core's legal and commonsense layers. A
// member of a legislative body introduces a bill, and the body enacts a statute bearing three norms: an obligation on
// landlords to keep a dwelling in repair, a permission for tenants to withhold rent once they have given notice of
// disrepair, and a prohibition on evicting without a court order; the behaviours the norms qualify are compared as
// obliged, allowed and disallowed. A landlord and a tenant play their roles in the context of a lease, which bears
// the tenant's obligation to pay rent and which the tenant promised; the tenant's written notice, borne by a letter,
// leads to the landlord's repairs. Each action has its actor, requirement, result and the moment it took up, and the
// moments are ordered in time.
//
// The data leaves the minimum counts of lkif__TemporalOccurrence_IMMEDIATELY_AFTER_TemporalOccurrence open: LKIF time
// is serial, so every moment has one immediately before and one immediately after it; the sample covers a finite
// stretch of time, so its first and last moments have no neighbour on one side.
//
// Run it against an empty database of Neo4j 5 or later, Enterprise Edition, for its node key and property existence
// constraints, e.g. with cypher-shell -f model.cypher. It merges every node and relationship, so it can be run again.

// The standards: their constraints

